{0 How to Drive [odoc]}

[odoc] is a command-line tool that turns the compiled modules and the [.mld]
pages of a project into documentation, one file at a time, through several
distinct phases. Like a compiler, it needs a {e driver} to call it in the right
order with the right arguments.

The {{!/odoc-driver/page-index}odoc-driver} package holds the {e reference
driver}, which is kept in step with [odoc]. Other drivers exist, such as
{{:https://dune.readthedocs.io/en/stable/documentation.html}dune} and
{{!/odig/page-index}odig}.

This document is for anyone who needs to call [odoc] themselves, you do not
need this document to {e use} [odoc] through a driver someone else wrote.
It covers:

- the model [odoc] uses to organise documentation: {{!units}trees};
- the four {{!pipeline}phases} and the command line of each;
- a {{!walkthrough}walkthrough} of what the reference driver does for one
  package, then what changes for {{!other}other kinds of packages} and for
  {{!ocamlorg}ocaml.org};
- the {{!conv}convention} that drivers and installed packages follow so that
  they work together.

The walkthrough names the modules of the reference driver that carry out each
step. Reading it next to the {{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib}library's own
documentation} is a good way to understand the whole process.

{1:units Trees of documentation}

The same documentation must work in at least two settings: a local switch, and
the {{:https://ocaml.org/packages}ocaml.org} hub for all
packages. In each setting the pages live at different places, and references
between them must still resolve.

[odoc] solves this with named groups of documentation, called {e trees}.
There are two kinds: {e page trees} and {e module trees}. [odoc] manages the
hierarchy inside a tree. The driver decides where each tree is rooted in the
overall hierarchy. A documentation author refers to another tree by its name,
not by its location.

Concretely, a tree is a name, a root {{!conv}identifier}, and a directory of
[.odoc] files. The driver passes a page tree as [-P name:dir] and a module
tree as [-L name:dir]. These names may be used in references to disambiguate
between modules or pages that share a name. The
reference driver makes one page tree per package, but mirrors the layout of
opam for the module trees - that is, sometimes a module tree for one library
may coincide with a module tree for another library.

{1:pipeline The pipeline}

Documentation is generated in four phases. A file may refer to a module that
depends on it, so the phases are separated to break this apparent cycle.

- The {e compile} phase turns [.cmi], [.cmt], [.cmti] and [.mld] files into
  [.odoc] files. Here [odoc] does work similar to the compiler's, such as
  computing the expansion of each module type. The dependencies between
  inputs are those of the compiled modules themselves.

- The {e link} phase turns [.odoc] files into [.odocl] files. It resolves the
  references written in documentation comments, and canonical paths.

- The {e indexing} phase gathers [.odocl] files into [.odoc-index] files, from
  which the sidebar and the search index are made.

- The {e generation} phase renders [.odocl] files, with the sidebar made in
  the phase before, as HTML, Markdown, man pages or LaTeX.

Three binaries are involved. [odoc] itself does
everything above. [odoc-md] turns a Markdown page into an [.odoc] file, since
[odoc compile] reads only [.mld]. [sherlodoc] builds the search database that
the generated pages query, and ships the JavaScript that queries it. A driver
that wants no search need not call it.

{2 The compile phase}

There is one command per kind of input: [odoc compile] for interfaces and
pages, [odoc compile-impl] for implementations, [odoc compile-asset] for
assets.

{3 Compiling interfaces and pages}

{@shell[
  $ odoc compile --parent-id <pid> -o <file>.odoc -L <lib1>:<dir1> -L <lib2>:<dir2> <input>
]}

- [<input>] is a [.cmi], [.cmt], [.cmti] or [.mld] file. Prefer [.cmti] files
  over the other compiled forms: they hold the documentation of the [.mli].

- [--parent-id <pid>] places the unit in the documentation hierarchy. It is a
  path of non-empty names separated by [/]. Together with the input's name it
  gives the unit its {e identifier}, which decides how references to the unit
  resolve and where its HTML goes. Units of one {{!units}tree} share a common
  prefix of their parent ids. The identifier is stored inside the [.odoc] file.
  The parent id used when creating local docs (e.g. [fmt/fmt.cli] for library
  [fmt.cli] in package [fmt]) is different from the parent id when creating
  docs for ocaml.org (e.g. [p/fmt/0.11.0/doc/fmt.cli])

- [-o <file>.odoc] is where the file goes. The name must be the input's name
  with the [.odoc] extension, prefixed by [page-] for an [.mld]. [odoc] finds
  a unit by that name, capitalised, and the prefix tells it what kind of unit
  to expect. Without [-o], [odoc] writes the file below [--output-dir] at the
  path given by [--parent-id].

  A tree's files may be rooted wherever the driver likes, since later phases
  are handed the directory. Within a tree they are not free. [odoc] resolves
  a path reference such as [{!/pkg/a/b/page-c}] by looking for
  [a/b/page-c.odoc] below the directory that [-P pkg:] names. A unit's path
  below that directory must therefore match its identifier below the tree's
  root. A [{!//a/b}] reference is resolved relative to the input file's own
  directory.

- [-L <name>:<dir>] names a library and the directory of its [.odoc] files.
  [odoc] searches the directory for the modules the input depends on, which
  are the ones [odoc compile-deps] lists for it. Each directory the compiler
  searched for [.cmi] files should therefore have a counterpart here. 

  The library names given via [-L] are written into the [.odoc] file. They are the libraries the
  compiler could see when the unit was built, and [odoc link] uses them later.
  Resolution that follows a path or a reference {e into} this unit carries on
  among these libraries. [-I <dir>] served a similar purpose but without naming a library, and so records nothing.
  It is what drivers passed before [-L] existed, and is deprecated for these
  two commands.

- [--warnings-tag <tag>] associates the contents of this unit with the tag.
  This is used when reporting warnings during linking, as it allows the user
  to distinguish between warnings that are caused by code from dependencies
  from warnings that are caused by the users code. The reference driver uses
  the {b package name} as the tag.

- [--enable-missing-root-warning] reports a root that cannot be found. It is
  off by default because the most-used driver for odoc at time of writing
  ([dune build @doc]) does not ensure that all roots are available. Any driver
  following the guidance of this document ought to enable this warning.

For example, for the [fmt.cli] library the reference driver runs:

{@shell[
  $ odoc compile ~/.opam/5.5.0/lib/fmt/cli/fmt_cli.cmti
      -o _odoc/lib/fmt/cli/fmt_cli.odoc
      -L fmt.cli:_odoc/lib/fmt/cli/ -L fmt:_odoc/lib/fmt/
      -L cmdliner:_odoc/lib/cmdliner/ -L stdlib:_odoc/lib/ocaml/
      --parent-id fmt/fmt.cli --warnings-tag fmt --enable-missing-root-warning
]}

{3 Compiling implementations}

{@shell[
  $ odoc compile-impl --parent-id <pid> --source-id <sid> -o impl-<file>.odoc -L <lib1>:<dir1> <input>.cmt
]}

The input is a [.cmt] file. The output name is [impl-] followed by the input's
name. [--parent-id] and [-L] mean the same as above, and the library names are
written into the unit in the same way. An implementation usually takes the
same parent id as its interface. [--source-id <sid>] is the identifier of the
page that will show the rendered source. Links to the source point there.

For the implementation of [fmt_cli] the reference driver runs:

{@shell[
  $ odoc compile-impl ~/.opam/5.5.0/lib/fmt/cli/fmt_cli.cmt
      -o _odoc/lib/fmt/cli/impl-fmt_cli.odoc
      -L fmt.cli:_odoc/lib/fmt/cli/ -L fmt:_odoc/lib/fmt/
      -L cmdliner:_odoc/lib/cmdliner/ -L stdlib:_odoc/lib/ocaml/
      --parent-id fmt/fmt.cli --source-id fmt/src/fmt.cli/fmt_cli.ml
      --enable-missing-root-warning
]}

The input is the [.cmt] beside the [.cmti] of the example above. The [-L]
arguments and the parent id are the same as the interface's, so the two units
sit together and resolve names the same way. The source id puts the rendered
source below the package's sources page. There is no [--warnings-tag], since
the driver ignores the warnings of an implementation.

{3 Compiling Markdown pages}

{@shell[
  $ odoc-md <file>.md -o <file>.odoc --parent-id <pid>
]}

[odoc compile] reads [.mld] and compiled interfaces, not Markdown, so a
Markdown page goes through the separate [odoc-md] binary. [--parent-id] and
[-o] mean what they do for [odoc compile], and [--output-dir] may be given
instead of [-o], with the same meaning. Everything after this phase treats
the result as any other page.

{@shell[
  $ odoc-md ~/.opam/5.5.0/doc/fmt/README.md
      -o _odoc/doc/fmt/page-README.odoc --parent-id fmt
]}

There is no search path to pass. A Markdown page has no references into
other units to resolve.

{3 Compiling assets}

{@shell[
  $ odoc compile-asset -o asset-<file>.odoc --parent-id <pid> --name <file>
]}

An asset is copied at generation time, but it needs a unit so that references
to it resolve. The unit is what makes [{image!logo.png}], and the other
{{!odoc_for_authors.media}media markup}, find the file and know its URL.
[--name <file>] is the name an author writes. [-o] and [--output-dir] mean
what they do for [odoc compile].

{@shell[
  $ odoc compile-asset --name ocaml_console.png
      -o _odoc/doc/brr/_assets/asset-ocaml_console.png.odoc
      --parent-id brr/_assets
]}

This is what the reference driver runs for the one asset [brr] installs. The
command does not name the file on disk, only its name and where the unit
goes. The file itself is read at generation time, by the command below.

{2 The link phase}

{@shell[
  $ odoc link <file>.odoc -o <file>.odocl
      -P <pkg1>:<dir1> -P <pkg2>:<dir2>
      -L <lib1>:<dir3> -L <lib2>:<dir4>
      -I <dir5> -I <dir6>
]}

- [-P <name>:<dir>] names a page tree and the directory of its [.odoc] files.
  References such as [{!/ocamlfind/index}] resolve against it.

- [-L <name>:<dir>] names a module tree likewise. References such as
  [{!/findlib.dynload/Fl_dynload}] resolve against it, and references by
  plain name search its directory.

- [-I <dir>] adds a directory to the search path without naming a tree. Two
  directories are on the path that were not passed: the directory holding the
  input [.odoc] file, and the [-P] directory of [--current-package].

- [--current-package <pkg>] names the package the unit belongs to, and a [-P]
  of that name must be given too. It is the tree a [{!//a/b}] reference is
  resolved in.

- [--warnings-tags <tag>] is repeatable, and says whose tagged warnings to
  report. See [--warnings-tag] above.

Odoc looks up a module by name in these directories, but not all at once.
What it tries first depends on whose name it is resolving.

A name that the unit's imports record with a digest is matched by that
digest, anywhere on the search path. The order below does not arise for it.
That covers most of what a signature mentions, because the compiler recorded
those modules as imports.

The order decides every other name. Those are a canonical path, a reference
written in a documentation comment, and an import that the compiler left
without a digest. Odoc searches in three steps. First it searches the
libraries that the unit on the command line recorded when it was compiled,
each in the directory its [-L] names. Then it searches the [-I] directories.
Only then does it search the rest, the [-L] directories included. A page
records no libraries of its own, so the first step is empty for a page, and
its search starts at [-I]. The first two steps hold what the compiler gave
the unit, as long as the driver passes its dependencies as [-I]. A name they
answer is therefore the module the unit was built against.

Resolution may follow a path or a reference {e into} another unit. It then
carries on among the libraries that unit recorded at compile time, which are
what its own compiler could see. This is what lets a page name a submodule of
one library rather than of its sibling. It is also what lets a module list
show each module's first paragraph as that module's own page shows it.

A module of a library that is in neither is found by the wider search. No
step of this search has a digest to tell same-named candidates apart. Where
a step finds two files of one name, odoc takes the first and warns. Authors
can avoid the ambiguity with a path reference that names the tree,
[{!/foo/A}] for the module [A] of library [foo].

This is the link phase. [odoc compile] searches the [-I] and [-L]
directories as one path, and matches by digest. A name it has no digest for
is a forward reference, not a search.

{{!scope}Step 3} of the walkthrough shows a link command from a real run. It
has the [-P], [-L] and [-I] arguments the reference driver computes for one
package.

No two directories given to [-P] may be nested or shared, and likewise for
[-L]. The two sets are checked separately, so a [-P] directory may contain a
[-L] one. Where even that is impossible, pass [--custom-layout], which
disables the check. Several libraries installed in one directory are the
usual reason.

{2 The indexing phase}

{3 Counting occurrences}

{@shell[
  $ odoc count-occurrences <dir1> <dir2> -o <file>.odoc-occurrences
]}

This counts how often each item is used by the implementations found below the
directories. The counts are used only by the JSON search index below.

{3 Indexing entries}

{@shell[
  $ odoc compile-index -o <file>.odoc-index --file-list <list> [--occurrences <file>]
]}

[<list>] names a file with one [.odocl] path per line. [odoc] builds one
hierarchy from these units' identifiers. Directories can be given instead
with [--root <dir>], but each root then gives a hierarchy of its own. That is
wrong when a package's pages and modules live in different directories. The
binary index does not use occurrence counts. Only the JSON form does, which
[--json] selects. Two further flags shape that form. [--simplified-json] cuts
it down to what a search page needs. [--wrap-json] wraps it in a JavaScript
assignment, so that a page can load it with a [<script>] tag. The reference
driver passes both for the index ocaml.org serves, and neither for its own.

{@shell[
  $ odoc sidebar-generate -o <file>.odoc-sidebar <file>.odoc-index
]}

This derives the sidebar from an index. With [--json] it writes the JSON form
used by ocaml.org.

The reference driver makes one index and one sidebar per package:

{@shell[
  $ odoc compile-index -o _index/fmt/index.odoc-index
      --file-list _index/fmt/index-inputs.txt
  $ odoc sidebar-generate -o _index/fmt/sidebar.odoc-sidebar
      _index/fmt/index.odoc-index
]}

The file list holds the [.odocl] files of the package, its pages and its
modules together, which is what puts them in one hierarchy.

{3 The search database}

{@shell[
  $ sherlodoc index --format js <file>.odoc-index -o <dir>/sherlodoc_db.js
  $ sherlodoc js <dir>/sherlodoc.js
]}

[sherlodoc] is a separate binary. The first command turns the index into the
database a page queries. The second writes the engine that queries it, which
every page shares. Both paths are then given to [odoc html-generate] as
[--search-uri], relative to the HTML directory. [--format marshal] writes the
database a server searches instead. [--favoured_prefixes] lists module
prefixes whose results come first.

{2 The generation phase}

{@shell[
  $ odoc html-generate <file>.odocl -o <html-dir>
      --sidebar <file>.odoc-sidebar
      --search-uri <path.js> --search-uri <path2.js>
]}

The output goes below [<html-dir>] at the path given by the unit's
identifier. A module becomes a directory, a page becomes a file. [--sidebar]
gives the sidebar to show. Each [--search-uri] is a JavaScript file, relative
to the HTML directory, that the page loads in a web worker. The worker
answers search queries. It receives the query as a string, and replies with a
string. That string holds a JSON list of objects, each with a [url] and an
[html] field.

{@shell[
  $ odoc html-generate-source --impl impl-<file>.odocl <source>.ml -o <html-dir>
  $ odoc html-generate-asset --asset-unit asset-<file>.odoc <file> -o <html-dir>
  $ odoc support-files -o <html-dir>
]}

These render a source file, copy an asset to its place, and write the
stylesheets and scripts every page needs. For [fmt_cli] and its source, the
reference driver runs:

{@shell[
  $ odoc html-generate _odocl/lib/fmt/cli/fmt_cli.odocl
      --sidebar _index/fmt/sidebar.odoc-sidebar
      --search-uri fmt/sherlodoc_db.js --search-uri sherlodoc.js
      -o _html --home-breadcrumb "Package index"

  $ odoc html-generate-source
      --impl _odocl/lib/fmt/cli/impl-fmt_cli.odocl
      ~/.opam/5.5.0/lib/fmt/cli/fmt_cli.ml
      --sidebar _index/fmt/sidebar.odoc-sidebar
      --search-uri fmt/sherlodoc_db.js --search-uri sherlodoc.js
      -o _html --home-breadcrumb "Package index"

  $ odoc html-generate-asset
      --asset-unit _odoc/doc/brr/_assets/asset-ocaml_console.png.odoc
      ~/.opam/5.5.0/doc/brr/odoc-assets/ocaml_console.png
      -o _html --home-breadcrumb "Package index"
]}

The source command takes two inputs: the [impl-] unit, which says where the
page goes and how to colour it, and the [.ml] file itself. The asset command
takes its unit and the file to copy. Neither needs a search path, because
nothing is resolved here.

Three more options matter to a driver. [--home-breadcrumb <name>] adds a
breadcrumb of that name, leading to the root of the sidebar. The reference
driver passes [--home-breadcrumb "Package index"] for every page, source and
asset it generates. [--remap-file <file>] gives the rules that rewrite links
into packages this run is not rendering, one [<identifier>:<url>] per line.
[--as-json] writes the page as JSON instead of HTML. The preamble, the
content, the breadcrumbs and the table of contents become separate fields.
That suits a site which puts odoc's output inside its own template, as
ocaml.org does. It is a second [html-generate] call rather than a different
one: the driver generates the HTML, then generates the JSON beside it.

{1:walkthrough What the reference driver does}

This section follows the reference driver through one ordinary package, then
looks at the cases that need something more. The driver is the
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib}[Odoc_driver_lib]} library, with two
executables on top: [odoc_driver] for a switch, [odoc_driver_voodoo] for
ocaml.org.

{2:standard A standard package: [fmt]}

The [fmt] package installs these files in the switch:

{v
lib/fmt/META
lib/fmt/fmt.cmi  fmt.cmti  fmt.cmt  fmt.mli  fmt.ml  fmt.cma ...
lib/fmt/cli/fmt_cli.cmi  fmt_cli.cmti  fmt_cli.cmt  fmt_cli.cma ...
lib/fmt/tty/fmt_tty.cmi  fmt_tty.cmti  fmt_tty.cmt  fmt_tty.cma ...
doc/fmt/odoc-pages/index.mld
doc/fmt/README.md  CHANGES.md  LICENSE.md
v}

The [META] file defines three libraries. [fmt] requires nothing; [fmt.cli]
requires [cmdliner] and [fmt]; [fmt.tty] requires [unix] and [fmt].

Running [odoc_driver fmt] documents it and the packages it depends on.

{3:installed Step 1: finding out what is installed}

Four things have to be known before anything is compiled. They are the same
four whether the modules belong to other people's packages or to the project
in front of you:

- {b What the modules are:} for each one, a compiled interface to read. A
  [.cmti] is best, because it carries the documentation written in the
  [.mli]. A [.cmt] or a [.cmi] will do when there is no [.cmti].

- {b How the modules are grouped into libraries.} A module is named within a
  library rather than within the world. Two libraries may each hold a module
  [Content]. To know which one a reference means, you must know the library
  the referring module belongs to. The grouping is not presentation. It is
  what makes the names unambiguous.

- {b What depends on what,} at both levels. A library's dependencies are the
  libraries whose modules it may name, and they fix the order the libraries
  are compiled in. A module's dependencies fix the order within a library.

- {b What else belongs to the package:} its name, its [.mld] pages, its
  Markdown, its assets, its source files, and its [odoc-config.sexp] if it has
  one.

None of that is specific to opam or to findlib. A project that documents
itself knows all four already, because its build system had to know them to
build the code at all. It may state them however it likes, in a [dune] rule
or in a list written by hand. The flags described in
{{!pipeline}the phases above} are the whole of the interface to [odoc]. The
rest of this step is how the reference driver recovers the same four facts
for a switch of packages it did not build. That is the awkward case rather
than the general one.

{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Packages}[Packages]} asks opam which packages
[fmt] depends on, and which files each package installed. It asks findlib
({{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Ocamlfind}[Ocamlfind]}) about every library:
its directory, its archives and its [requires]. From that it builds one
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Packages.t}[Packages.t]} per package. For
[fmt] this holds three libraries. [odoc classify] finds the modules of each
library's archive. [odoc compile-deps] then gives each module the digest of
its interface and the modules it depends on. The [Packages.t] also holds the
page [index.mld], the Markdown files, and the contents of [odoc-config.sexp]
if the package has one. Those two [odoc] commands exist for this situation.
They answer the grouping and the dependency questions from the installed
files alone, when there is nothing better to go on. A driver that is handed
the answers has no need of them.

A [Packages.t] is made for the packages of this run, and for no others. A
run documents one package, or a few, or everything installed. Either way,
its libraries require libraries that are not in it. The dependencies of a
voodoo job are the clearest case: earlier jobs compiled them, and their
[.odoc] files are on disk already. Three facts about such a library are
still needed. They are the directory of its [.odoc] files, the package that
provides it, and what it requires in turn.
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Odoc_units_of}[Odoc_units_of]} looks them up
while it builds the units, from findlib and from opam's record of installed
files, and remembers the answers. So step 1 is not the only place a driver
learns what is installed. It is the place it learns everything about what it
is going to build.

Nor can a driver assume findlib is there to be asked. Findlib is configured by
[ocamlfind], which is a package like any other. A switch holding only the
closure of a package built with dune need not contain it, and ocaml.org
documents packages in exactly such switches. Findlib would have supplied two
facts: a library's [requires], and its object directory relative to the
switch, [lib/fmt/cli] for [fmt.cli]. That directory is the one the later steps
mirror. Both facts are then read from what is on disk instead. Every installed
package ships the [META] that names its libraries, their directories and their
[requires], and reading one needs no configuration. The compiler's own
libraries ship no [META] of their own. They are below the directory [ocamlc
-where] prints, one subdirectory each, and a sub-library shares its parent's
directory: [compiler-libs.common] is in [compiler-libs], [threads.posix] in
[threads]. A library that nothing says anything about is still compiled
against the standard library. Assuming that much is what lets its modules
resolve the signatures they are constrained by.

All of this is detective work, and it is detective work only because nothing
installed states the facts outright. The driver reads the modules of a library
back out of the library's archive. It reads what the library requires out of a
file that [ocamlfind] defined. The
{{:https://github.com/ocaml/RFCs/blob/master/rfcs/ocamlib.md}library linking
proposal} would take most of it away. A library would be a directory. It would
hold [M.cmi], [M.cmx], [M.cmti] and [M.cmt] for each of its modules, beside a
single archive named [lib]. The modules of a library would then be the ones in
its directory, and no module could be in two libraries. A library's name would
be its path below a root of [OCAMLPATH], with the separators written [.]. Name
and directory would determine each other, with no file to consult. The
library's dependencies would be recorded in the archive as it is built, and
read back with [ocamlobjinfo]. That is the [requires] field of a [META], moved
into the archive. The proposal names documentation systems among the tools the
convention is meant to serve.

It remains a proposal. The document was merged into [ocaml/RFCs] in 2020, and
the update to it has been open since 2021. The compiler support exists as a
partial implementation on a branch, and no switch is laid out that way today.
A driver would still have to work out what the proposal does not describe.
That is which modules of a library depend on which, and which pages, assets
and sources belong to a package.

{3 Step 2: deciding identifiers and locations}

{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Odoc_units_of}[Odoc_units_of]} turns each
[Packages.t] into an {{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Odoc_unit.pkg}[Odoc_unit.pkg]}:
the list of units to build, grouped by library, with everything the later
steps need.

Each unit gets an {e identifier} and a {e location}. They follow different
layouts, and keeping them apart is the point.

The identifier follows the {{!conv}convention}: the package's pages under the
package name, a library's modules under [<pkg>/<lib>]. It decides the URLs.

{v
fmt/page-index          the package's index page, from doc/fmt/odoc-pages/index.mld
fmt/fmt/Fmt             the module Fmt of library fmt
fmt/fmt.cli/Fmt_cli     the module Fmt_cli of library fmt.cli
fmt/fmt/page-index      the library's landing page, written by the driver
v}

The location mirrors the switch. A library's [.odoc] files are written at the
path of its object directory, and a package's pages under [doc/<pkg>]:

{v
_odoc/lib/fmt/fmt.odoc            beside lib/fmt/fmt.cmi in the switch
_odoc/lib/fmt/cli/fmt_cli.odoc
_odoc/doc/fmt/page-index.odoc     the package's index page
_odoc/doc/fmt/fmt/page-index.odoc the landing page of library fmt
v}

The same paths hold under the directory for [.odocl] files. See
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Odoc_unit}[Odoc_unit]} for the functions that
compute both layouts.

Mirroring the switch has two consequences. First, libraries installed in one
directory share one odoc directory. A single [-I] finds all of them, exactly
as a single [-I] found their [.cmi] files. Second, every location is a
function of a name. A library's location comes from its findlib directory, a
package's from its name. A run that documents a package on its own can
therefore find what earlier runs built, with no record of them. This is how
ocaml.org works, see {{!ocamlorg}below}. The odoc directory may even be the
switch prefix itself, in which case each [.odoc] file lands beside the [.cmti]
it was compiled from.

{3:scope Step 3: the search path and the scope}

For each library, [Odoc_units_of] computes its {e dependency cone}: the
library itself and, transitively, what it requires, each with the directory of
its [.odoc] files. This is what the compiler searched when it built the
library. For [fmt.cli] it is [fmt.cli], [fmt], [cmdliner] and the standard
library. The cone is given to [odoc compile] with [-L], which finds the files
and writes the names into the units. It is given to [odoc link] with [-I].

For each package it computes the {e reference scope}: the [-P] and [-L]
arguments its units are linked with. Every unit of the package gets the same
scope. It is not the transitive closure:

- [-L]: the package's own libraries, the libraries they require directly, and
  the libraries named in [odoc-config.sexp];
- [-P]: the package itself, the packages providing those libraries, and the
  packages named in [odoc-config.sexp].

The page the driver writes for a library is linked with that library's [-I]
as well. It is a page and has no modules of its own, but it shows a synopsis
for each module. It must reach what those synopses name.

For [fmt], with [cmdliner] not installed as a dependency, the link command of
[Fmt] is:

{@shell[
  $ odoc link _odoc/lib/fmt/fmt.odoc -o _odocl/lib/fmt/fmt.odocl
      -P fmt:_odoc/doc/fmt -P ocaml-compiler:_odoc/doc/ocaml-compiler
      -L fmt:_odoc/lib/fmt/ -L fmt.cli:_odoc/lib/fmt/cli/ -L fmt.tty:_odoc/lib/fmt/tty/
      -L stdlib:_odoc/lib/ocaml/ -L unix:_odoc/lib/ocaml/unix/
      -I _odoc/lib/fmt/ -I _odoc/lib/ocaml/
      --current-package fmt --warnings-tags fmt --custom-layout --enable-missing-root-warning
]}

Opam's record of installed files says which package provides a library.
[stdlib] and [unix] are in [lib/ocaml], installed by [ocaml-compiler]. A
library stands in this list only if it is being built in this run, or its
[.odoc] directory exists already. Otherwise the libraries it requires stand
in for it. That is what handles an alias, such as [num] requiring only
[num.core], or [threads.posix] requiring only [threads]. It is also what
drops a library nobody has documented, here [cmdliner], which then
contributes nothing.

{3 Step 4: compiling}

{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Build}[Build]} drives the whole run, package
by package, in the order the packages were found. Each one goes through
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Build.compile_package}[Build.compile_package]},
which compiles its libraries with
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Compile.compile_lib}[Compile.compile_lib]}
and its pages with
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Compile.compile_pages}[Compile.compile_pages]}.
That function is the whole of the compile phase for one package, and it is
what {{!ocamlorg}a voodoo job} calls too.

Order matters twice, and it is a requirement rather than an optimisation.
[odoc compile] computes each module's expansions. A dependency that has not
been compiled yet is one whose signature cannot be expanded, and no later
phase repairs that.

Within a library, a module is compiled after the modules it depends on, so
that their [.odoc] files exist when [odoc] looks for them. The order is keyed
by interface digest rather than by module name. The driver groups the
library's interfaces by the digest [odoc compile-deps] reported, and compiles
the group an import names before the module that imports it. An import whose
digest matches no interface of this library belongs to a library compiled
earlier, and is found through the [-I] path. Keying on the digest is what
lets a virtual library and its implementations be compiled as one thing,
since they share one interface. See {{!virtual}below}.

Between libraries, a library is compiled after the libraries it requires:
[fmt.cli] after [fmt], [fmt] after [stdlib]. [Build] keeps a table of the
libraries compiled so far, and compiles a library's requirements first,
recursively. Requiring is a relation between libraries, not packages. No
library requires itself even indirectly, so this always ends.

The compile command for [fmt_cli.cmti] was shown {{!pipeline}above}. Its [-L]
arguments name the four libraries of the cone with their directories.
[lib/fmt/fmt.odoc] is already there because [fmt] was compiled first, and
[lib/ocaml/stdlib.odoc] because [ocaml-compiler] was an earlier package. The
names go into [fmt_cli.odoc], so a later run resolving something into that
module knows the four libraries it may look in.

{3 Step 5: linking}

{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Compile.link}[Compile.link]} links every unit
of the package with the package's scope. The units of a library, its page
included, also get that library's [-I] path. Linking needs the [.odoc] files
of everything the scope names, so [Build] makes sure each package in the scope
has been compiled before it links. For [fmt] that is already so. When it is
not, see the {{!forward}forward references} below.

{3 Step 6: index, sidebar, search and HTML}

{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Compile.generate}[Compile.generate]} finishes
the package. It writes the list of the package's [.odocl] files and runs [odoc
compile-index] on it. Pages and modules then form one hierarchy, although they
are in different directories. From the index it makes the sidebar with [odoc
sidebar-generate] and the search database with [sherlodoc]. Then it runs [odoc
html-generate] on each unit, and [odoc html-generate-source] on each
implementation whose source was found. The output:

{v
_html/fmt/index.html                the package page
_html/fmt/fmt/index.html            the library landing page
_html/fmt/fmt/Fmt/index.html        the module
_html/fmt/src/fmt/fmt.ml.html       its rendered source
_html/fmt/sidebar.json              the sidebar, for ocaml.org
_html/fmt/sherlodoc_db.js           the search database
_html/index.html                    the list of documented packages
v}

Warnings [odoc] printed while compiling and linking [fmt] are shown at the end
of the run. Those of dependency packages are not: the driver is for fixing the
documentation of the packages you asked for.

{2:other Other kinds of packages}

{3 Several libraries in one directory}

The compiler's libraries [compiler-libs.common], [compiler-libs.optcomp] and
the others all live in [lib/ocaml/compiler-libs]. A module of one may use a
module of another without the [META] file saying so. The compiler never
noticed, because one [-I] covered them all. Mirroring the switch gives the
driver the same behaviour: their [.odoc] files share
[_odoc/lib/ocaml/compiler-libs], and one [-I] finds all of them. Their [-L]
roots then name the same directory, which is why the driver always passes
[--custom-layout].

{3:virtual Virtual libraries}

[checkseum] is a virtual library with two implementations, [checkseum.c] and
[checkseum.ocaml]. An implementation ships its modules as [.cmt] files only.
The [.mli], and the documentation in it, belong to the virtual library, which
ships the [.cmti]. Both have the same interface digest.

The driver documents each implementation by compiling the virtual library's
[.cmti] again, under the implementation's own parent id. [checkseum.cmti] is
compiled three times, under [checkseum/checkseum], [checkseum/checkseum.c]
and [checkseum/checkseum.ocaml].
{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Packages.remap_virtual}[Packages.remap_virtual]}
makes the substitution when the virtual library is part of the run. The
virtual library may instead have been built by an earlier run, as on
ocaml.org. [Compile.compile_lib] then looks along the implementation's [-I]
path for a [.cmti] of the same name with the right digest. So that one is
there to be found, the driver copies the [.cmti] of every library that ships
no archive next to that library's [.odoc] files. Virtual libraries are among
those.

The landing page the driver writes for a library always lists its modules
with path references. It writes [{!modules: /checkseum.c/Checkseum}] rather
than [{!modules: Checkseum}], for every package and not only this one.
Checkseum is the reason: three modules named [Checkseum] are in scope there,
and a plain [{!Checkseum}] would pick one of them at random.

{3:siblings Libraries that share module names}

A package may ship two libraries that define the same module names on purpose.
[eliom] is the example: [eliom.client] and [eliom.server] mirror each other,
and that symmetry is the point of the framework. Nothing in the reference scope
tells them apart, so a plain [{!Eliom_content}] in a doc comment is
ambiguous. Odoc warns and takes the first it finds. An author who means one
of them writes a path reference, [{!/eliom.client/Eliom_content}].

A module's own documentation is not affected. Its [-I] holds its library's
dependency cone, and never both siblings, and odoc searches that first. The
types it shows, the modules its signature mentions and the references its
comments make therefore all stay on the side they were written on.

Nor is what a page of the package says about those modules, although a page
has no cone of its own. Two things resolve among the libraries the module
recorded when it was compiled. One is a reference into the module, such as
[{!/eliom.client/Eliom_content.Html}]. The other is the first paragraph a
module list shows beside each entry. What remains ambiguous is only a name
that says nothing about where it comes from, the plain [{!Eliom_content}]
above.

{3 Alias libraries}

Some findlib packages provide no modules and exist only to require others.
[num] requires [num.core], [threads.posix] requires [threads], and
[compiler-libs] requires [compiler-libs.common]. A [META] file naming one of
these means the libraries it requires. The driver follows the requirement
when it computes the scope and the compile order. [sexplib.num] requires
[num], so it gets [-L num.core] and is compiled after it.

{3 Archives that hold other archives}

A module can be in more than one archive, and [odoc classify] says so,
because it is the truth. The OCaml distribution ships its middle end twice:
as [ocamlmiddleend.cma], of 77 modules, and inside [ocamloptcomp.cma], which
holds those 77 and 49 more. A module is documented once, so the driver gives
each one to the largest library that holds it. The bundling archive is also
the one that ships as a library. Findlib declares [compiler-libs.optcomp] and
has no name at all for the middle end, so that is the library a reader can
depend on. A library left with nothing of its own is not documented, and the
driver says which.

This only arises where libraries are named after their archives, which is
what the driver falls back to when a package installs no [META]. Where the
[META] is there, the archives it does not name are not libraries, and the
question does not come up. Under the
{{:https://github.com/ocaml/RFCs/blob/master/rfcs/ocamlib.md}library linking
proposal} it could not arise at all: one directory, one library, one archive.

{3 Libraries that were not built}

A [META] names the libraries a package can install, not the ones it did. A
package built without an optional dependency still declares the sub-library
it skipped. [fmt] declares [fmt.cli] when [cmdliner] was absent, and [logs]
declares [logs.lwt], [logs.fmt] and the rest. The directory the [META] names
is then not there. The driver documents nothing for that library, and logs
that it found nothing. The same happens for a [META] that points outside its
own package, as [topkg] points at [topkg-care].

This is worth knowing when you compare two runs. A library missing from the
output may mean the package was built differently, not that the driver
dropped it.

{3:forward Forward references through [odoc-config.sexp]}

A package's pages may refer to a package built on top of it. [eio]'s
documentation points at [Eio_main.run], and [eio_main] depends on [eio].
[eio] declares this in [doc/eio/odoc-config.sexp]:

{v
(packages eio_main bigstring)
(libraries unix)
v}

This adds [eio_main]'s pages and libraries to [eio]'s scope. But [eio_main]
comes later in the build, so its [.odoc] files do not exist when [eio] would
be linked. [Build] handles this by compiling first and linking later. Before
it links a package, it compiles every package the scope names. So it compiles
[eio], then [eio_main], then links [eio], and later links [eio_main] in its
turn. Compiling never needs the forward reference, since it follows
[requires] only, and linking only needs the [.odoc] files. This is the same
split ocaml.org makes between the two phases, see below.

{3 Packages with no libraries, or no pages}

A package with pages but no libraries, or with libraries but no pages, is
ordinary. The driver writes a landing page for every package that does not
ship an [index.mld], and one for every library. Every package therefore has
an entry point. Modules whose names contain [__] are hidden. They are
compiled, because other modules depend on them, but not linked or rendered.

{3 Dependencies documented elsewhere}

By default every dependency is documented too, so that links into them work.
With [--remap], dependencies are compiled but not linked or rendered. They
must still be compiled, because the selected packages' units depend on their
[.odoc] files. Links into them are rewritten to point at their documentation
on ocaml.org.

{2:ocamlorg ocaml.org: one package per job}

ocaml.org documents every version of every package. Each is built by a
separate job that runs [odoc_driver_voodoo] on the output of [voodoo-prep]: a
directory [prep/universes/<u>/<pkg>/<version>] holding the files the package
installed. [voodoo] is the name of the part of
{{:https://github.com/ocurrent/ocaml-docs-ci}ocaml-docs-ci} that does that
preparing, and the driver is named after it. The [<u>] is a {e universe}. The
same version of a package can be built more than once, against different
versions of its dependencies, and each such build is one universe. The build
ocaml.org shows by default for a version is the {e blessed} one, and the
driver is told so with [--blessed].

The dependencies are installed in the job's switch. Their [.odoc] files are
the ones earlier jobs wrote into the odoc directory, at the locations the
mirror layout predicts. The switch holds that package's closure and no more,
so findlib is often not there to be asked. The facts then come from the
[META] files and the compiler, as {{!installed}step 1} describes.

{{!/odoc-driver/Odoc_driver_lib.Voodoo}[Voodoo]} reads the prepared package
into a [Packages.t]. Everything after that is the same code as for a switch,
applied to one package. That the two drivers share the compile step is not
tidiness. A package's own libraries require one another as often as libraries
of different packages do. Compiling them in whatever order they were listed
in leaves unresolved what the later ones should have expanded. Identifiers
start with [p/<pkg>/<version>] for a blessed build, and
[u/<universe>/<pkg>/<version>] for any other.

The job scheduler needs the compile and link phases separately, for the same
forward-reference reason as above. [--actions compile-only] compiles a
package. [--actions link-and-gen] links and renders it, once the packages its
scope names are compiled. Occurrence counts, and the JSON search index that
uses them, are produced at the end of the link job.

{2:scheduling What waits for what}

The walkthrough gives the order for one package. A driver needs the rule
behind that order, because the rule also says what may run at the same time:

- A module waits for the modules it imports. [odoc compile-deps] gives the
  digest of each import, and the module with that digest is compiled first.
- A library waits for the libraries it requires. One of its modules may
  import a module of another library, which must be compiled by then.
- A page waits only for its own [.mld] file. It is compiled with no search
  path at all.
- Linking a unit waits for the compilation of its own package, and of every
  package its scope names.
- A package's index waits for the [.odocl] files it lists. The sidebar and
  the search database wait for the index.
- Rendering a unit waits for its [.odocl] file and for the sidebar.

Nothing waits for a link. [odoc link] reads what [odoc compile] wrote, and
writes somewhere else. The graph is therefore acyclic, and a driver may run
as much of it at once as it has processors.

The reference driver holds this graph as the shape of its own code. It forks
a fiber for each unit. It keeps a table of promises, so that a module
already under way is awaited rather than compiled twice. It hands every
command to a pool of workers, which bounds how many run at once. Nothing in
it holds the graph as data.

{3 Writing the build instead of running it}

Deciding a command and running it are two steps, so a driver can write the
commands out instead of running them. [odoc_driver --makefile <file>] writes
the whole build as rules for [make], and runs none of it. [make -j] then
schedules what the driver would have scheduled itself, and a second [make]
rebuilds only what changed.

Make schedules the work, so the rules must state the graph as data. A module
names the [.odoc] files of the imports it waits for. A stamp file stands for
each set that a later phase waits for: one per library, and one per package.

Finding out what is installed stays outside the file. [odoc classify],
[odoc compile-deps], opam and findlib all run while the file is written,
because they decide what the units are. The file is therefore valid for the
switch as it was at that moment.

{1:conv Convention for installed packages}

For drivers and installed packages to work together, both follow the
convention below. The reference driver implements it. Other drivers should
give the same results.

{2 Trees and their roots}

Each installed package [<p>] defines one page tree, named [<p>], with root
identifier [<p>]. Each of its libraries [<l>] defines one module tree, named
[<l>], with root identifier [<p>/<l>].

A package [foo] with libraries [foo] and [foo.bar] thus defines three trees.
They are the page tree [foo], the module tree [foo] rooted at [foo/foo], and
the module tree [foo.bar] rooted at [foo/foo.bar]. Another package refers to
them at link time with [-P foo:<dir>], [-L foo:<dir>] and
[-L foo.bar:<dir>]. Each [<dir>] holds that tree's [.odoc] files.

The driver may root the trees differently, as long as references still
resolve. ocaml.org puts the version in the root identifier.

{2:pkg_units The units of a package}

The module units of a package are the compiled modules of the libraries it
installed below [lib]. The page units are the [.mld] files below
[doc/<p>/odoc-pages], and the [.md] files directly below [doc/<p>], which go
through [odoc-md] rather than [odoc compile]. The asset units are the other
files below [doc/<p>/odoc-pages], and all files below [doc/<p>/odoc-assets].

The driver adds pages of its own, which a package cannot supply:

- one for the package, unless it ships [doc/<p>/odoc-pages/index.mld], listing
  its pages and, per library, its modules;
- one for each library, listing that library's modules;
- one at [<p>/src], introducing the rendered sources, when any module of the
  package has an implementation to render.

Each is an [.mld] that the driver writes to a scratch directory and compiles
like any other page. The library pages and the source page carry two tags.
[@toc_status hidden] keeps them out of the table of contents.
[@order_category] decides where they sit among the package's pages.

{2 Parent ids}

Interface and implementation units have the root of their library tree as
parent id: [<p>/<l>].

A page found at [doc/<p>/odoc-pages/<relpath>/<name>.mld] has the parent id
[<p>/<relpath>]. An asset found there likewise. An asset found at
[doc/<p>/odoc-assets/<name>] has the parent id [<p>/_assets].

Implementation units have the source id [<p>/src/<l>/<name>.ml].

{2 The reference scope}

A package's units are linked with the scope described in the
{{!standard}walkthrough}. [-L] names its own libraries, the libraries they
require directly, and those named in [odoc-config.sexp]. [-P] names the
package itself, the packages providing those libraries, and those named in
[odoc-config.sexp]. The transitive closure is deliberately not included. To
refer to something in a transitive dependency, name it in the [META]
[requires] field or in [odoc-config.sexp]. This is the driver side of the
{{!odoc_for_authors.reference_scope}reference scope} that authors see.

The dependency cone is a different matter. It must reach everything a unit's
modules depend on, transitively, because expansions and source links do. The
driver gives it to [odoc compile] as [-L], naming each library, and to
[odoc link] as [-I]. Odoc searches the recorded libraries, and then the [-I]
directories, before the rest of the scope. Where a name is in both, a unit
therefore gets the module it was built against. The library names are written
into the units. That holds for another run resolving into them as well, and
not only for the unit on the command line. The page the driver writes for
a library is given that library's cone for the same reason.

{2 [odoc-config.sexp]}

A package [<p>] may install [doc/<p>/odoc-config.sexp], holding
s-expressions:

- [(packages p1 p2 ...)] adds the page trees [p1], [p2], ... to the scope, and
  the module trees of the libraries those packages provide;
- [(libraries l1 l2 ...)] adds the module trees [l1], [l2], ... to the scope,
  and the page trees of the packages providing them.

{2 Ordering the generated pages}

The convention introduces directories, one per library. An author cannot set
their order in the sidebar with [@children_order]. In the pages it writes
itself, the driver uses the [@order_category <name>] tag to help [odoc] sort
them. The resulting order is:

- first, pages in the order given by their parent's [@children_order];
- then by kind: pages and directories, then modules, then implementations,
  then everything else;
- then by [@order_category], compared as a string, a page that sets none
  compared as the string [default];
- within a category, by [@short_title] where the page sets one, otherwise by
  the page's own name, or for a page named [index] by the name of its
  directory;
- ties are left in the order the units were given.

[@order_category] may change and is not for authors to use. It is for the
pages a driver generates.
